"An authorization graph connects principals, policies, actions, and resources to explain possible or effective access.\n\nUse when: Review privilege paths and unexpected resource access. Explain the effect of grants, denies, and policy conditions.\n\nAvoid when: Only a compact comparison of role permissions is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Distinct node types identify identities, policy attachments, and resources. Labeled edges state membership, assumption, grant, or resource relationships. Conditions and explicit denies remain visible rather than hidden in edge color.\n\nBuild: Choose an authorization system and effective-access question. Collect policy, membership, and resource evidence from the same scope. Model condition-dependent and denied paths explicitly. Arrange principals and resources on opposite sides with policy nodes in clear intermediary lanes.\n\nCheck: A grant edge is not mistaken for effective access without policy evaluation. Conditions, boundaries, and explicit denies follow the selected system. Evidence time and unverified permissions are stated. Long policy notes do not cross principal or resource labels.\n\nPitfalls: Flattening all policy types into one additive permission set. Assuming an identity can assume a role because a similarly named grant exists.\n\nExample: A workload identity may assume a role that can read a bucket only under a specified resource condition.\n\nAlternatives: Role-permission matrix, Identity federation diagram."
